\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


A nonconstant meromorphic function $f$ has bounded value distribution if for every $r>0$ there is a finite constant $C_f(r)$, uniform over disc centres and finite target values $w$, bounding the number of solutions of $f(z)=w$ in every disc of radius $r$, counted with multiplicity. One may equivalently impose such a bound at one fixed positive radius.
For entire $f$, let $C_f(r)$ denote the least such bound.

\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
(a) If an entire function has bounded value distribution, must $C_f(r)=O(r)$ as $r\to\infty$, and hence must $f$ have at most exponential type?
(b) For a meromorphic function, is it enough to impose the uniform disc-count bounds for only three distinct finite values $w$ in order to force bounded value distribution for all values? \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}{[S2]}
The paragraph also records Wittich's characterisation for the equation $y^{(n)}+a_1(z)y^{(n-1)}+\cdots+a_n(z)y=0$ with entire coefficients: all its solutions have bounded value distribution exactly when all coefficients are constant. Update 2.28 reports an affirmative answer to (a) and a negative answer to (b). \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Does uniformly bounded local valence force a linear bound as discs grow, and can checking only three target values suffice?

